// `x·to_string()` on values the compiler cannot prove are strings. Every one of
// these reaches the DOM in a view.

☉ rite t_int() -> String! {
    ⤺ (42)·to_string();
}
☉ rite t_zero() -> String! {
    ⤺ (0)·to_string();
}
// `(∅)·to_string()` is NOT here, and the client emits it in a hundred places.
// The interpreter rejects it outright — "no method 'to_string' on type
// Discriminant(14)" — and the WASM backend cannot even tell the case apart:
// `None` and the integer zero are both 0 in a uniform i64 value model, so one
// of `(∅)·to_string()` and `(0)·to_string()` has to be wrong whatever is
// chosen. A probe cannot hold both; the divergence is recorded instead.
☉ rite t_literal() -> String! {
    ≔ s = "hello";
    ⤺ s·to_string();
}
☉ rite t_via_helper() -> String! {
    ⤺ helper()·to_string();
}
☉ rite helper() -> Any! {
    "from a helper"
}
☉ rite t_concat() -> String! {
    ⤺ "n=" + (7)·to_string();
}
☉ rite t_bool() -> String! {
    ⤺ (1 == 1)·to_string();
}

☉ rite main() {
    println(t_int());
    println(t_zero());
    println(t_literal());
    println(t_via_helper());
    println(t_concat());
    println(t_bool());
}
